;; FH-REACT-004 — the bridge selects a frame; it never creates one.
;;
;; A migration seam is exactly where a framework is tempted to be helpful about
;; frames, and exactly where being helpful is worst: a bridge that created a
;; frame when it could not find one would split an application into two runtimes
;; that share no state and agree about nothing, and the symptom would be
;; "my dispatch does nothing" rather than an error.
;;
;; The row therefore turns on OWN-PROPERTY PRESENCE rather than truthiness. The
;; distinction is not pedantry: `frame={null}` and no `frame` at all read the
;; same under any truthiness test, and they are opposite intentions. A caller
;; who wrote the property meant to state a target, so a target that names
;; nothing is a failure; a caller who omitted it meant the ambient chain.
;;
;; `:attribution` is the half a delegating implementation gets wrong. Scoping
;; through a shared provider makes it easy to let the provider's own diagnostic
;; escape — which blames `frame-provider` at a call site that never mentioned
;; one, on precisely the migration seam this verb exists to simplify.
{:fh/id "FH-REACT-004"
 :fh/law
 "An own `frame` prop SCOPES an already-live frame and creates, refreshes and destroys nothing; own-property presence decides, so an explicit nil, a malformed target and a target naming no live frame each fail loud and self-attributed to the bridge rather than falling through to the ambient chain, while an OMITTED prop resolves the frame ambiently and fails with the ordinary no-frame-context diagnostic under no frame boundary at all."

 ;; Every typed failure on this row names the bridge, not the shared provider.
 :attribution v/->react

 ;; The live frame the scoping arm selects, and the state it reads through so a
 ;; render proves the frame actually arrived rather than merely being accepted.
 :frame-id  :to-react/host
 :db        {:person {:name "Ada"}}
 :query     [:person/name]
 :props     {:salutation "Hello"}
 :rendered  "Hello, Ada"

 ;; A frame is SCOPED, never owned: the count of live frames does not move
 ;; across an export, a render, and an unmount.
 :creates-no-frame true

 ;; An OWN `frame` property naming nothing usable. Each is a stated target, so
 ;; each fails rather than falling through.
 :stated-but-unusable
 [{:note  "an explicit nil — the case truthiness gets wrong"
   :target nil
   :error :rf.error/no-frame-context}
  {:note  "a malformed target"
   :target 42
   :error :rf.error/bad-frame-provider-arg}
  {:note  "a keyword naming no live frame"
   :target :to-react/never-created
   :error    :rf.error/frame-provider-frame-absent
   :recovery :ensure-or-create-the-frame}]

 ;; The OMITTED property — the sole ambient case.
 :ambient
 {:note  "no `frame` property: the ordinary chain resolves it"
  :rendered "Hello, Ada"}

 :no-boundary
 {:note  "omitted, and no frame boundary above the exported component at all"
  :error :rf.error/no-frame-context}}
